{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1427501-combat-question","id":1427501,"title":"Combat question","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-12-06T17:01:09-08:00","id":1427501,"title":"Combat question","content":"Hi everyone,<br /> <br /> One of my players likes using guns. How would you go about ammo tracking?<br /> <br /> Rapid Reflex's allows for a no reloading penalty, but there was no information in the core book about ammo consumption.<br /> <br /> Do fire arms in the game follow the same rules as real life? Does Ammo capacity in a pistol work the same and do Resources and Contacts advantage level corresponds to the number of reloads you can have? (Low levels in them mean fewer rounds)<br /> <br /> So if my player as an example says. &quot;I have a pistol with, (I'm not a gun guy so let's say, 9 rounds) 9 rounds to the magazine&quot; <br /> <br /> &quot;My contact rate is 3 so before the game starts I would like my character to have 10 reloads with means 90 rounds at the start&quot;<br /> <br /> Is that how it works? And if my players go for All Out Attack, would they have to -9 from Thier ammo total to reload the gun.<br /> <br /> What about using it in ranged combat? Does each damage from the margin mean that's how many bullets hit? What about the bullets that miss?<br /> <br /> Thanks for reading","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26360-toa_kiril","name":"Toa_Kiril","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"27"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-12-06T21:30:45-08:00","id":1427520,"title":"","content":"Well, Vampire is more of a narrative game. So I wouldn't track ammo per se, but I might say that on a Massy Critical the kindred shoot their entire clip on the target or something. In a nutshell, they will have to reload in an appropriately dramatic moment <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24461-godforsaken","name":"Godforsaken","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24461_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"150"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-12-07T04:34:29-08:00","id":1427558,"title":"","content":"Outside of a few low capacity guns (Let's say 6 shots or less) and full auto, most firearms are going to be able to go through a whole fight without worrying about reloading. V5's combat is meant to be vague, and the weapon table has the idea that a gun that shoots small bullets fast is virtually the same as a gun that shoots big bullets slow.<br /> <br /> <br /> Ammo capacity isn't dependent on cost of the gun. If anything, the limiting factor for how-much-ammo you should take with you is concealment.IE you're gonna get all sorts of negative attention if you walk around with enough ammo for a seige. If the game is set in a gun liberal place like the US or Afghanistan, If you have one dot of resources, you probably don't have a good gun unless you stretch; but that's more measured in reliability or long range accuracy, and you're unlikely to use any exotic ammo. Ammo itself isn't outrageously expensive. If you have two dots you can comfortably have a decent gun or, if you lived in a more standard gun environment (You need a liscence) you could get a gun but not anything like a heavy machinegun or anti-material rifle or luxury blaster; that's three dot territory/with some corruption involved for the military stuff.<br /> <br /> <br /> You should actually be trying to avoid scenarios where you're mowing down wave after wave of enemies. Best use of a gun in Vampire is intimidation. The moment you need to start shooting you've made a mistake.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25794,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","user":"MyWifeIsScary","date":". 12-07-2020, 07:36 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-12-07T05:18:16-08:00","id":1427566,"title":"","content":"When it comes to some combat mechanics like All Out Attack, is there a limit to how many times a player is allowed to do it? Otherwise it's a plus 1 dice everytime they attack.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26360-toa_kiril","name":"Toa_Kiril","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"27"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-12-07T09:35:04-08:00","id":1427606,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Toa_Kiril</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1427501-combat-question#post1427501\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hi everyone,<br /> <br /> One of my players likes using guns. How would you go about ammo tracking?<br /> <br /> Rapid Reflex's allows for a no reloading penalty, but there was no information in the core book about ammo consumption.<br /> <br /> Do fire arms in the game follow the same rules as real life? Does Ammo capacity in a pistol work the same and do Resources and Contacts advantage level corresponds to the number of reloads you can have? (Low levels in them mean fewer rounds)<br /> <br /> So if my player as an example says. &quot;I have a pistol with, (I'm not a gun guy so let's say, 9 rounds) 9 rounds to the magazine&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;My contact rate is 3 so before the game starts I would like my character to have 10 reloads with means 90 rounds at the start&quot;<br /> <br /> Is that how it works? And if my players go for All Out Attack, would they have to -9 from Thier ammo total to reload the gun.<br /> <br /> What about using it in ranged combat? Does each damage from the margin mean that's how many bullets hit? What about the bullets that miss?<br /> <br /> Thanks for reading</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> So a couple of things...<br /> <br /> Regarding attacks in general, it's an abstraction. Tying 'how many bullets hit or miss' to damage is fairly meaningless in the grand scheme of things. Narrate appropriately to what you feel goes well with the damage and the target of the attack; four damage on someone with 9 health is narratively different than 4 damage on someone with 5 health, for example.<br /> <br /> The guns are an abstraction, and I'd personally shy away from players dumping real-world gun minutiae into the game. There was a reason huge tables of guns with different stats were dropped from V5. It does address this in the section on reloading (see below) though, so you have some set abstractions there to play around with.<br /> <br /> On reloading: Pg. 302 of the core has the particulars, if you need and want to use those, for tracking ammunition.<br /> <br /> For Contacts? That's ultimately up to you to determine what's fair and within the scope of what they're asking the contact to do, it's not just 'the player gets to declare something'. The narrative of it matters, what the player wants and how hard the Contact might find that (contraband bullets like hollowpoints, for a narrative thought, for example); a 3 dot Contact is pretty well-connected, so getting extra generic bullets shouldn't be super difficult in general. But I'd cap how many they could get to a reasonable amount, especially if they're trying to make it untraceable to them.<br /> <br /> All out attack would just empty the gun per the normal rules; it doesn't incur any penalty by itself other than what is in the description of all-out attack. If they reloaded the gun on the next turn, they'd take the -2 reload penalty to any actions they take (you would do the reload as part of declaration, so that you know how much extra action penalties they are going to have).<br /> <br /> There is also no limit to how many times that they can do it, but that character is probably very dead after the second time they do it, if not the first. They get +1 damage (not +1 to their dice pool), and then they can't defend themselves at ALL. So if they roll 6 successes on the enemy and the enemy rolls 3? They are going to take that 3 damage because they can't defend themselves. Then anyone else attacking them gets to roll vs. Difficulty 1, because the character who did all-out attack can't defend themselves there as well. It is a supremely sub-optimal option unless you absolutely have to alpha strike a single person who is by themselves.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1808-elmerg","name":"elmerg","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"539"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-12-07T13:03:40-08:00","id":1427624,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>elmerg</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1427501-combat-question?p=1427606#post1427606\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They get +1 damage (not +1 to their dice pool), and then they can't defend themselves at ALL.</div> </div> </div> </div>so if i am reading this and the core rule book correctly, using all out attack once mean they cannot defend for the rest of the combat? because both passages don't at any point include the phrasing &quot;until next turn&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26360-toa_kiril","name":"Toa_Kiril","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"27"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-12-07T13:48:58-08:00","id":1427631,"title":"","content":"Nah, so like, if memory serves V5 combat goes like this<br /> <br /> Dave is going to shoot Kevin with his twin pistols while diving over a couch, Kevin is going to shoot Dave with his hip fired machinegun while doing the pen'n paper equivelent of m1+W.<br /> They both roll something (composure?) plus firearms, but both dave and kevin are willing to be reckless with their bullets for a better chance of hitting and suppressing their opponents (and because that's apparently cool and this is the tone I picked up reading the rules; A round of shooting also includes the shooting characters moving around evasively, probably in black leather trenchcoats) Going &quot;All out&quot; just means they're happy to use extra bullets and maybe do some collateral.<br /> <br /> Dave gets two more successes than Kevin, so Dave does two damage, and then we'll say his pistols are +1 weapons so he does three damage to Dave. Kevin doesn't hit Dave at all because he was probably flinching from getting shot; You guys narrate what happens however you like.<br /> <br /> I think if you then had Dave's friend Jessica charge out from behind Kevin with a Samurai sword, Kevin would roll firearms again and she'd roll strength+melee. If Kevin wins he sprays her before she swings, if she wins he continues to shoot like a stormtrooper (or she slices his bullets mid air or whatever's fun) Every character picks someone to engage with and if they're picked twenty times they can kill twenty people if they win twenty times... I think. Might be some multi-combat rules to make that a little more difficult. Been a while since I read the book.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-12-07T14:31:57-08:00","id":1427634,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Toa_Kiril</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1427501-combat-question?p=1427624#post1427624\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> so if i am reading this and the core rule book correctly, using all out attack once mean they cannot defend for the rest of the combat? because both passages don't at any point include the phrasing &quot;until next turn&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>It would be until end of turn by default, because it says the bonus is to a dice roll/action. It also has the rule as to what happens after that turn: if the person attacking fails (by rolling no successes whatsoever), then anyone attacking them the next turn also gets a +1 die bonus. So no, he doesn't lose his defense forever, just the turn he's using All Out Attack (and because V5's conflict happens simultaneously, he's already going to be engaged with everyone already attacking him). My mention of 'second turn' was addressing your what-if of using AOA every turn.<br /> <br /> By default, it doesn't need to say 'until end of turn' like D&amp;D unless absolutely necessary, because the assumption is that everything is just going to be one turn/action by default for the normal Conflict rules, unless otherwise specified, since other things WILL say 'next action' or 'for X rounds' or whatever.<br /> <br /> Also, there are quite a few unfinished/conflicting conflict examples in the book. I got with Karim on Facebook and asked him a number of questions, and built a Combat Primer for V5 that talks about things in a bit more easy-to-grok terms. You can find it here (<a href=\"https://www.v5homebrew.com/wiki/Combat_Primer\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.v5homebrew.com/wiki/Combat_Primer</a>) if you like to read it. It covers bot the standard and advanced combat options.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1427501-combat-question?p=1427631#post1427631\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Nah, so like, if memory serves V5 combat goes like this<br /> <br /> Dave is going to shoot Kevin with his twin pistols while diving over a couch, Kevin is going to shoot Dave with his hip fired machinegun while doing the pen'n paper equivelent of m1+W.<br /> They both roll something (composure?) plus firearms, but both dave and kevin are willing to be reckless with their bullets for a better chance of hitting and suppressing their opponents (and because that's apparently cool and this is the tone I picked up reading the rules; A round of shooting also includes the shooting characters moving around evasively, probably in black leather trenchcoats) Going &quot;All out&quot; just means they're happy to use extra bullets and maybe do some collateral.<br /> <br /> Dave gets two more successes than Kevin, so Dave does two damage, and then we'll say his pistols are +1 weapons so he does three damage to Dave. Kevin doesn't hit Dave at all because he was probably flinching from getting shot; You guys narrate what happens however you like.<br /> <br /> I think if you then had Dave's friend Jessica charge out from behind Kevin with a Samurai sword, Kevin would roll firearms again and she'd roll strength+melee. If Kevin wins he sprays her before she swings, if she wins he continues to shoot like a stormtrooper (or she slices his bullets mid air or whatever's fun) Every character picks someone to engage with and if they're picked twenty times they can kill twenty people if they win twenty times... I think. Might be some multi-combat rules to make that a little more difficult. Been a while since I read the book.</div> </div> </div> </div>Basically, a pretty succinct overview. Though if both were going all out for the +1 damage, they'd both be hitting each other, it's just possibly that Dave would do more damage (because basically both are ignoring safety and not bothering to dodge). Otherwise, pretty accurate to that scenario if they don't both All Out Attack.<br /> <br /> On the last part, though, you wouldn't roll separately against Jessica; as V5 has just the one opposed conflict roll. In that instance, Kevin has two options:<ul><li>If Kevin wants to try to damage Jessica back as well, have had to split his dice pool to attempt to do so, so he'd be splitting his Composure+Firearms (or whatever pool was narratively appropriate) against Jessica's attack pool and against Dave's Composure + Firearms pool.</li> <li>On the flipside, he could dedicate his whole attack against Dave first, and then use his normal dodge/defense pool against Jessica, rolling Dex + Athletics -1 against her (in V5, you don't have to abort to dodge, you are always assumed to be able to defend yourself unless something actively prevents it). This is something that is implied by the text but badly explained, and we got clarification from Karim on Facebook as to how it should run.</li> </ul>Dice pool splitting is another sub-optimal option unless you're just THAT much better at your thing than your opponents' defense pool.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1808-elmerg","name":"elmerg","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"539"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-12-08T13:47:08-08:00","id":1427747,"title":"","content":"WW games tend to treat logistical planning and resource tracking like Schrodinger treats cats, it both is and isn't tracked.<br /> <br /> Ammunition is boring to track in a game like Vampire, but that moment when you run out of ammo can be amazing in game play. You get scenes like the first Matrix where both Neo and Agent Smith run out of ammo at the same time or like in the first Dirty Harry movie where Harry is actually out of ammo but mentally wins the intimidation match with the injured robber reaching for his weapon simply by giving a minor informercial about the gun and asking if he is feeling lucky. So I wouldn't suggest dismissing ammo tracking completely, just because of the potential RP scenes alone.<br /> <br /> My advice is to consider the ordinance the characters are using. If you are keeping the weaponry to revolvers, derringers, simple shot guns, basic rifles, etc. Then each weapon has 10 rounds or less and thus is really no different than tracking your bloodpool or willpower. Where things get troublesome is when the characters start wielding high capacity military grade guns, at which point you just have to ask the gun user to track &quot;bursts&quot; of fire and reverse math out how many rounds the character can fire the weapon on a single clip and then multiply out how many clips the character carries around.<br /> <br /> Yeah that can add to a characters book keeping, but that is no where near as bad as the Resources &amp; Money issue in the games. Vampire A has Resources ** here in the United States, he then travels to Africa for some archeology fun, but due to the local economy his Resources ** functions as Resources ****. Then as a follow up he meets a business partner in Dubai whose economy makes his Resources score act more like Resources *. This is assuming of course that the vampire even has access to international banking, which means he ends up with a null Resource score if he crosses the wrong boarder.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}